WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.551
In the three years since the death of Sherlock Holmes, my dear wife had also died.

2
00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:10.741
Thanks to my friendship with the great detective, I continued to be interested in crime.

3
00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:16.487
For my own amusement and to distract myself from my grief, I often followed interesting cases.

4
00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:19.868
But of course I was never successful in solving any crimes.

5
00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:26.927
I think that Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard also missed Holmes because he sometimes visited me to discuss his latest cases.

6
00:00:26.928 --> 00:00:34.717
One spring evening our topic of conversation was Ronald Adair's murder, or as the newspapers called it, the Park Lane mystery.

7
00:00:34.718 --> 00:00:39.371
Everyone in London was talking about this case, which was baffling the police.

8
00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:44.315
"There's no question that Adair was murdered," Lestrade said as we sat in my study.

9
00:00:45.136 --> 00:00:48.514
"But I can't figure out how it was done or who did it."

10
00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:54.307
"Perhaps it would help if you went over the details of the case, starting with what you know about the victim."

11
00:00:54.308 --> 00:00:59.003
I poured a cup of tea for each of us. "That's what Mr. Holmes would do."

12
00:00:59.004 --> 00:01:05.828
Lestrade nodded. "Ronald Adair was a wealthy young man who spent his time with the best members of society. One

13
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:14.017
thing that makes this case so puzzling is that he didn't seem to have any enemies—male or female. Everyone liked him." "That

14
00:01:14.018 --> 00:01:17.661
is unusual," I said. "Go on." "Adair

15
00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:22.167
belonged to three London card clubs, where he regularly played whist." Lestrade

16
00:01:22.168 --> 00:01:25.811
explained that whist is a game played with a partner. On

17
00:01:26.694 --> 00:01:34.526
the night Adair died, he and his whist partner, Colonel Sebastian Moran, played against Daniel Murray and Sir John Hardy. All

18
00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:38.694
three men said that Adair never lost much money at cards. "I

19
00:01:38.695 --> 00:01:43.136
remember reading that in the newspaper stories about the murder," I said. "I

20
00:01:43.137 --> 00:01:48.750
also read that Adair and Colonel Moran had recently won a large amount of money while playing cards. Now

21
00:01:48.751 --> 00:01:50.894
tell me about Adair's death." "After

22
00:01:50.895 --> 00:01:56.051
playing cards Adair returned home at around ten o'clock," Lestrade said. "His

23
00:01:56.052 --> 00:02:01.033
mother and sister, with whom he lived on Park Lane, were out visiting relatives. Anticipating

24
00:02:01.034 --> 00:02:05.518
his arrival, the maid had lit a fire in the fireplace in his study. The

25
00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:08.856
room is on the second floor at the front of the house. Because

26
00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:13.125
the fireplace smoked, she opened a window. She

27
00:02:13.126 --> 00:02:20.094
heard Adair enter his study, but she didn't hear anything else until Adair's mother and sister came home around eleven-thirty." Lestrade

28
00:02:20.095 --> 00:02:25.909
said that when Mrs. Adair went to say good night to her son, she found his study door locked. She

29
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.725
called for a servant, who forced the door open. Adair

30
00:02:28.726 --> 00:02:32.676
had been shot to death, but there was no weapon in the room. On

31
00:02:32.677 --> 00:02:37.828
his desk were some piles of money and a list of club members' names and some numbers. "It

32
00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:43.316
sounds like Adair was trying to figure out how much money he had won and lost at cards," I said. "But

33
00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:46.461
why had he locked the door from the inside?" Lestrade

34
00:02:47.358 --> 00:02:51.213
shook his head. "That's one of the mysteries. It's

35
00:02:51.214 --> 00:02:54.709
unclear whether Adair or the murderer locked the door. One

36
00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:59.205
theory was that the murderer had climbed through the window and locked the door. But

37
00:02:59.206 --> 00:03:04.890
if you examine the scene, you'll see that there was no way anyone could access the window." "Park Lane

38
00:03:04.891 --> 00:03:07.911
is such a busy street," I said. "There are

39
00:03:07.912 --> 00:03:10.806
pedestrians on that street even at night. I'm surprised

40
00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:14.203
that no one saw the murderer or heard the shot." "That's another

41
00:03:14.204 --> 00:03:19.492
part of the mystery!" Lestrade threw up his hands in exasperation. "If only

42
00:03:19.493 --> 00:03:22.832
Sherlock Holmes were here, he'd figure it out." *** The

43
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:27.315
next evening I decided to stroll down Park Lane to examine the scene of the crime. A

44
00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:34.121
few people were looking up at a window while they listened to a man talking about his own theories on the case. "That

45
00:03:34.122 --> 00:03:37.393
must be the window of Adair's study," I thought. "And

46
00:03:37.394 --> 00:03:41.860
Lestrade was right—it's completely inaccessible from the ground. There's

47
00:03:41.861 --> 00:03:45.575
no tree or downspout that anyone could climb to reach it." I

48
00:03:45.576 --> 00:03:50.157
listened to the man talking about the case but quickly decided his theories were absurd. From

49
00:03:50.158 --> 00:03:54.070
his manner and his dress, I thought he might be a private detective. Turning

50
00:03:54.071 --> 00:03:58.305
away, I bumped into an elderly man who was standing behind me on the sidewalk. "Oh,

51
00:03:58.306 --> 00:04:03.810
excuse me!" I said, knocking some old books he was carrying to the ground. As

52
00:04:03.811 --> 00:04:08.798
I bent to pick them up, I noticed that one was The Origin of Tree Worship .

53
00:04:09.862 --> 00:04:14.187
Wondering if this fellow was a collector of used books, I tried to apologize.

54
00:04:14.188 --> 00:04:18.279
But the old man snarled at me and hurried away with his precious volumes.

55
00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.641
I glanced up at Adair's window again, but feeling more puzzled than ever, I headed back home.

56
00:04:23.642 --> 00:04:27.821
I hadn't been sitting in my study five minutes when my maid announced a visitor.

57
00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:33.014
To my astonishment, it was the book collector carrying about a dozen volumes with one arm.

58
00:04:33.015 --> 00:04:37.722
"You're surprised to see me, sir," he said in an odd, croaking voice.

59
00:04:37.723 --> 00:04:41.305
His weather-beaten face peered out from a frame of white hair.

60
00:04:41.306 --> 00:04:44.796
I nodded. "May I ask how you knew where I lived?"

61
00:04:44.797 --> 00:04:51.266
"Well, I have a conscience, sir, and I thought I'd apologize to such a kind gentleman," he said.

62
00:04:52.445 --> 00:04:55.539
"I hobbled after you and saw you enter this house.

63
00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.039
If I was a bit gruff in my manner when we met earlier, I didn't mean any harm.

64
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.245
I'm much obliged to you for picking up my books." "You're

65
00:05:03.246 --> 00:05:06.318
making too much of a small thing," I said. "Your

66
00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:08.714
books must be very important to you." "Yes,

67
00:05:08.715 --> 00:05:11.567
indeed! Books are my life." The

68
00:05:11.568 --> 00:05:15.474
old man's eyes lit up, and he put down his pile of treasures. I

69
00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:19.531
began to wonder how I was going to get this stranger out of my house. "You

70
00:05:19.532 --> 00:05:24.844
probably don't realize it, but I'm a neighbor of yours," he said, looking around the room. "You'll

71
00:05:24.845 --> 00:05:29.694
find my little bookshop at the corner of Church Street, and I'd be very happy to see you there. Maybe

72
00:05:31.737 --> 00:05:34.436
you collect books yourself, sir?" He

73
00:05:34.437 --> 00:05:41.146
pulled a book from his pile. "Here's British Birds , for example, in fine condition and a bargain.

74
00:05:41.147 --> 00:05:43.308
As are all my other books!

75
00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:47.442
Five volumes would just fill up that empty space on the shelf over there."

76
00:05:47.443 --> 00:05:53.080
I turned my head to look at the bookcase, and when I looked back, Sherlock Holmes was standing there, smiling at me.

77
00:05:53.081 --> 00:05:57.030
I stared at him for a few seconds in utter amazement.

78
00:05:57.031 --> 00:06:03.185
Then, for the first time in my life, I fainted.

